# Feature 8: Wire ask command (ask.go) - COMPLETED

## Summary
Wired the ask command for single-phase skill execution with full provider integration.

## Changes Made

### 1. Updated ask.go (internal/presentation/cli/commands/ask.go)
- Changed command signature from `ask <question>` to `ask <skill> <question>` to accept skill name
- Added `--phase` flag to specify which phase to execute (defaults to first phase)
- Added `--stream` flag for streaming output support
- Integrated with Container for accessing SkillRegistry and ProviderRegistry
- Implemented skill lookup by ID or name via GetSkill() and GetSkillByName()
- Added getPhaseToExecute() to get specified phase or first phase
- Built prompt from phase template with input substitution
- Created provider router for model selection based on profile
- Execute requests via provider's Complete() or Stream() method
- Display formatted response with token counts
- Modernized profile validation using slices.Contains()

### 2. Updated chat.go (internal/presentation/cli/commands/chat.go)
- Moved initChatService() function from ask.go (which was removed in rewrite)
- Added required imports for provider and config packages

### 3. Updated commands_test.go (internal/presentation/cli/commands/commands_test.go)
- Updated TestNewAskCmd_Structure to check for new --phase and --stream flags
- Updated TestAskCmd_Validation test cases with new 2-argument format (skill + question)
- All ask-related tests pass

## Key Features
1. Load skill from registry by ID or name
2. Execute first phase (default) or specified phase via --phase flag
3. Route to provider based on profile (cheap/balanced/premium)
4. Display response with optional streaming (--stream flag)
5. Proper error handling for missing skills, phases, and providers

## Testing
- All ask-related tests pass: TestAskCmd_Validation, TestNewAskCmd_Structure, TestValidateAskProfile
- Build succeeds for entire project

## Note
Pre-existing test failures in run command tests (TestRunCmd_Validation) are due to run.go being wired by another feature. Those tests now fail because they pass dummy skill names that don't exist. This is outside the scope of Feature 8.
